nodecode-gbrain is a rewrite of aean0x's aean0x/hermes-gbrain-retrieval-reflex:
https://github.com/aean0x/hermes-gbrain-retrieval-reflex
commit 78516ca629588612ebfc0a7ca86944fbb63d7dd8
MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 aean0x (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the whole plugin, in Common Lisp, against Nodecode's cell
contract. The original is a Hermes `pre_llm_call` hook that calls a GBrain
server's HTTP MCP endpoint before each turn and injects the top pages it
answers with. The same two calls (`volunteer_context` for named entities,
`query` for subject matter), the same merge over one page per slug, the same
ranking (entity hits first, then query hits by score, capped), the same
preview cleaning and the same byte ceiling on the block are kept, and the
same "no pages" turns stay silent. What changed is where they come from and
who reads them: the rewrite reads the section `gbrain` instead of the
environment, reaches the model through a `:round` hook that records the block
in the first request of a turn, and also offers the same lookup as a tool
(`gbrain_recall`), a slash command (`/gbrain`) and a Lisp verb
(`gbrain:recall`), which the original did not have.

What was left out: the optional `.gbrain-resolve.sock` resolve IPC fallback
(the HTTP endpoint is the path the original called primary, and an IPC frame
is not something a cell should open on a guess); the per-lookup audit marker
file, which was a diagnostic for the original's own author and wrote page
slugs and scores to disk; the `$HERMES_HOME/.env` scrape, replaced by the
`token_file` setting, which can name that file or the token GBrain mints; and
the Hermes-specific trivial-turn regular expression, replaced by a small
word list and a length check. The original's history window, message
truncation and injected-block stripping are kept.
